-## New deterministic evidence
-
-- A cancellation-ignoring resolver proves connect returns at the configured resolution deadline before release; teardown reaches `closed`, the retained task is reported, and release restores the full resource baseline.
-- A cancellation-ignoring upload source proves upload and transport close both return before release; the retained task is reported, and release restores sessions, channels, sockets, tasks, allocations, and buffers to zero.
-- A cancellation-ignoring external signer proves cancellation retains task ownership until explicit release and then returns to baseline.
+- Closed the round-8 teardown-admission defect by separating teardown-owned timeout races from the 64-slot ordinary async-operation registry.
+- The single teardown owner has a fixed two-slot registry: one socket-close race and one automatic-task-join race. Reserved admission cannot fail because ordinary work is saturated, and unfinished teardown work remains visible in `LibSSH2OwnedResourceSnapshot`.
+- `SSHTCPConnection.close()` is no longer skipped through a suppressed ordinary-registry admission failure. The socket close remains bounded and exact-once.
+- Added a deterministic regression that fills the ordinary registry to 64 while socket readiness ignores cancellation, initiates transport close before releasing those operations, proves bounded close and exactly one socket close, then proves late readiness performs no bridge read/write and all owned resources eventually return to zero.
+- Preserved the adapter-only libssh2/OpenSSL boundary and all four explicit M3-deferred `unsupported`/`notReported` states owned by `TASK-260728-3cveay`; no deferred value is fabricated.
 
